/// A `<length>`: value + unit (SVG ch.7.10).
///
/// The atomic coordinate quantity used across coordinates, layout and
/// painting. `auto` carries no meaningful magnitude (the field is zero).
///
/// Layout: `u32` unit + `f32` value = 8 bytes, zero padding (the `u32`
/// unit is what keeps the pair naturally 4-byte aligned).
enum Length {
    /// Auto — size determined by content.
    Auto(f32),

    /// Logical pixels.
    Px(f32),

    /// Percentage of the parent's corresponding dimension.
    Percent(f32),
}

/// A point in user space — an offset in the x and y directions.
///
/// Represents a translation (ch.7.4 `translate(tx ty)`) or any coordinate
/// pair; both components are `<length>`s so they may mix units.
struct Offset {
    dx: Length,
    dy: Length,
}

/// A rectangle: origin + size.
///
/// The same four numbers as a `viewBox` (SVG ch.7.7 `<min-x> <min-y>
/// <width> <height>`) or a nested viewport's `x y width height` (ch.7.9) —
/// but deliberately **not** the viewport itself: a viewport also has
/// min/max size constraints (`MinMaxSize`) plus an origin, and we don't
/// model one. The canvas stays fixed within its element's bounds; UI
/// elements go through the layout algorithm and relative offsets.
struct Bounds {
    dx: Length,
    dy: Length,
    width: Length,
    heigth: Length,
}

/// Image sizing behavior within its layout box.
///
/// Mirrors CSS `object-fit` and SVG `preserveAspectRatio` semantics.
enum ImageFit {
    /// Stretch to the box, ignoring aspect ratio (CSS `fill`).
    Fill,

    /// Largest size that fits inside the box (CSS `contain`, SVG `meet`).
    Contain,

    /// Smallest size that covers the box (CSS `cover`, SVG `slice`).
    Cover,

    /// Keep intrinsic size, no scaling (CSS `none`).
    None,
}

/// Payload for `Attr::TRANSFORM` — a 2D affine matrix in SVG ch.7.6
/// `matrix(a, b, c, d, e, f)` field order (24 bytes), field-for-field
/// identical to SVG's `SVGMatrix`. Values are `<number>`s (unitless,
/// ch.7.6) acting on user-space coordinates — never device pixels:
///
/// ```text
/// | a c e |     maps (x, y) → (a·x + c·y + e, b·x + d·y + f)
/// | b d f |
/// | 0 0 1 |
/// ```
///
/// `Default` is the identity transform (`a = d = 1`), not all-zeros — an
/// all-zero matrix collapses everything to the origin.
///
/// Note: 24 bytes exceeds the 12-byte inline limit, so `TRANSFORM` needs
/// the side-table route (same allowlist caveat as `SPACING`).
struct Transform {
    a: f32,
    b: f32,
    c: f32,
    d: f32,
    e: f32,
    f: f32,
}

/// SVG `translate(<tx> [<ty>])` — payload for `Attr::TRANSFORM_TRANSLATE`
/// (8 bytes, inline).
///
/// Components are `<number>`s in **user-space units** (see the module doc
/// on numbers vs. lengths); SVG omits `ty` when it is 0, which maps to
/// `dy = 0`.
struct Translate {
    /// Translation along x (`tx`).
    dx: f32,
    /// Translation along y (`ty`); defaults to `0`.
    dy: f32,
}

/// SVG `scale(<sx> [<sy>])` — payload for `Attr::TRANSFORM_SCALE`
/// (8 bytes, inline).
///
/// Components are `<number>`s (unitless scale factors); SVG omits `sy`
/// when it equals `sx`, which maps to `sy = sx`.
struct Scale {
    /// Horizontal scale factor (`sx`).
    pub sx: f32,
    /// Vertical scale factor (`sy`); SVG defaults it to `sx`.
    pub sy: f32,
}

/// SVG `rotate(<angle>)` — payload for `Attr::TRANSFORM_ROTATE`
/// (4 bytes, inline).
///
/// The wire value is in **radians** (Canvas2D convention; the SVG
/// presentation attribute's degrees are converted at the edge). The
/// rotation origin comes from `Attr::TRANSFORM_ORIGIN`, SVG's
/// `rotate(angle, cx, cy)` form.
struct Rotate(
    /// Rotation angle in radians, clockwise-positive in screen space.
    f32,
);

/// SVG `skewX(<angle>)` + `skewY(<angle>)` — payload for
/// `Attr::TRANSFORM_SKEW` (8 bytes, inline).
///
/// The two skew axes share one payload (SVG's transform *list* applies
/// them together). Values are in radians, like [`Rotate`].
struct Skew {
    /// `skewX` angle in radians — shears x by `tan(angle)` per unit y.
    x: f32,
    /// `skewY` angle in radians — shears y by `tan(angle)` per unit x.
    y: f32,
}
